"""Beginner introductions and real-world contexts for every published school unit.
Detailed topic explanations and examples are drawn from the existing authored manifests.
"""
# key: introductory explanation, causal model, real-world connection, figure kind.
DATA={
'y7/maths/numbers-i':(
'Whole numbers help us count objects, arrange equal groups and describe repeated events. A factor divides a number exactly; a multiple is made by multiplying it by a whole number. A prime has exactly two positive factors, so 1 is neither prime nor composite. Start by making small lists before using faster methods.',
'Prime factorisation describes a number using prime building blocks. For the HCF, keep only factors shared by both numbers, with the smaller repeated count. For the LCM, include enough prime factors to build either number. Powers mean repeated multiplication, not multiplication by the exponent. In BIDMAS, multiplication and division have equal priority and run left to right; addition and subtraction do too.',
'If 24 pens and 36 clips must be packed into identical kits with none left over, the greatest possible number of kits is HCF(24,36) = 12. Each kit has 2 pens and 3 clips. If lights flash every 24 s and 36 s, their next shared flash is after LCM(24,36) = 72 s. The same numbers answer different questions.',None),
'y7/maths/geometry-i':(
'Geometry describes position, shape and size. A point marks a position, a line segment joins two points, and an angle describes a turn between directions. Shape names tell you properties, such as equal sides or parallel edges; a drawing that looks convincing is not a proof. Read the stated properties before calculating.',
'Perimeter counts distance around a boundary, while area counts how many square units cover a region. Splitting a polygon into triangles explains its interior-angle sum: (n − 2) × 180°. Dividing that total by n finds one interior angle only when the polygon is regular. For compound shapes, include every exposed boundary in the perimeter and avoid counting shared internal edges.',
'A rectangular garden 4 m by 3 m needs 14 m of fencing but has 12 m² of ground to cover. A 5 m by 2 m garden needs the same fence length and has a different area, 10 m². A builder ordering paving and fencing must distinguish these quantities and units.',None),
'y7/maths/numbers-ii':(
'A signed number tells you which side of a chosen zero you mean. Positive and negative do not mean good and bad: they can represent above and below sea level, or money added and money owed. On a number line, values increase towards the right. −8 is less than −3 even though its distance from zero is greater.',
'Adding a signed change moves from a starting value. Subtracting a change means adding its opposite. Multiplying by a negative number reverses sign, so two sign reversals give a positive result. Brackets matter in powers: (−3)² = 9, whereas −3² = −9 because the square applies before the leading minus sign.',
'A temperature of −2 °C rising by 5 °C becomes +3 °C. An account at −£4 receiving £10 becomes +£6. These are starting values plus signed changes; neither story asks you to ignore the minus sign or replace every negative number with its magnitude.',None),
'y7/maths/algebra-i':(
'A variable is a symbol for a quantity that can change or is not yet known. An expression describes a value; an equation states that two values are equal. A table, a formula and a graph can describe the same relationship. Decide first what each variable measures, including its units.',
'Coordinates are ordered pairs (x,y): horizontal position first, then vertical position. Direct proportion has a constant ratio y/x and can be written y = kx; its graph goes through the origin. Inverse proportion has a constant product xy and can be written y = k/x for nonzero x. An increasing relationship is not automatically direct proportion, especially if it includes a fixed starting amount.',
'At £4 per notebook, three notebooks cost £12: the point (3,12) belongs to y = 4x. A £4 delivery fee would change the total rule and break direct proportion. For a fixed 12 m² rectangle, width 3 m needs length 4 m and width 6 m needs length 2 m: the product, not the ratio, stays constant.','functions'),
'y7/maths/algebra':(
'An unopened box can stand for an unknown but fixed number of objects. Let x be the number of pens in a pen box and y the number of clips in a clip box. x + 3 means one x-sized quantity plus three individual pens. It does not say that x itself is three.',
'A coefficient counts copies of a variable part: 3x means x + x + x. Like terms have exactly the same variable part, including powers. x² means x × x and is different from both x and x³. Identical bags represent brackets: three bags of x + 2 contain three x quantities and six loose items. The outside multiplier reaches every term inside.',
'If a school packs two identical bags, each with x pens and 3 loose pens, the total counts can be written 2(x + 3) = 2x + 6 when counting all items. If a cost p belongs to each pen, distinguish the cost expression from the object count: variables must keep their stated meaning.',None),
'y7/maths/numbers-iii':(
'A fraction compares a part with a specified whole, and its denominator tells you how that whole is divided into equal parts. A decimal is another way to express a number; a percentage means per hundred. A ratio compares quantities and needs its order and total parts interpreted carefully.',
'Equivalent fractions multiply numerator and denominator by the same nonzero factor. For addition, use equal-sized parts through a common denominator; multiplication has a different rule. A percentage increase or discount is measured from a stated original amount, not from the new amount. A map scale compares lengths in the same units, not areas by the same scale factor.',
'Sharing £20 in the ratio 1:3 means four total parts worth £5 each, giving £5 and £15. A 25% discount on £40 removes £10 and leaves £30. On a 1:1000 map, 3 cm represents 3000 cm = 30 m; using centimetres on one side and metres on the other without conversion changes the meaning.',None),
'y9/science/circulatory-system':(
'Your cells need a continuous supply of oxygen and nutrients and a way to remove waste. Blood is the transport fluid, blood vessels provide routes, and the heart produces the pressure differences that keep it moving. The heart is a muscular organ, not a store of oxygen and not the place where air enters the body.',
'The circulation has two connected circuits. The right side sends blood to the lungs; the left side sends blood to the body. Atria receive blood and ventricles pump it out. Valves guide flow in one direction as pressure changes. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, while veins return it; those names describe direction, not oxygen content.',
'When you exercise, muscles use oxygen faster and produce more carbon dioxide. Heart rate and blood flow can increase to support transport. The left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall because it normally generates higher pressure for the systemic circuit. Both ventricles must nevertheless move the same average volume over time in a stable circulation.','circulation'),
'y9/science/breathing-system':(
'Breathing moves air into and out of the lungs. Gas exchange transfers oxygen and carbon dioxide between air and blood. Respiration is a chemical process in cells that transfers energy from food; it is not another name for moving air. Start by separating these three processes.',
'Air travels through branching airways to alveoli, small air spaces surrounded by capillaries. During inhalation, contraction of the diaphragm and external intercostal muscles increases thoracic volume, lowering lung pressure relative to the atmosphere so air flows in. Quiet exhalation is mainly passive recoil. Diffusion then moves gases down their partial-pressure gradients across the thin exchange surface.',
'After running, you may breathe faster and deeper. This helps maintain gas gradients as cells consume more oxygen and make more carbon dioxide. A large total alveolar area and a thin moist barrier support exchange; ventilation and blood flow both help renew the gases on opposite sides.','breathing'),
'y9/science/gas-laws':(
'A gas consists of particles moving in many directions. Pressure describes force per area arising from interactions with a boundary; volume is the space occupied; temperature on the kelvin scale relates to mean particle kinetic energy. These are different quantities, so choose the variables and the conditions before naming a gas law.',
'For a fixed amount of ideal gas, keeping temperature constant gives Boyle’s inverse pressure–volume relationship. Keeping pressure constant gives Charles’s direct volume–temperature relationship. Keeping volume constant gives the pressure law, relating absolute pressure to absolute temperature. Use kelvin for temperature ratios and absolute pressure, not a gauge reading. Real gases can depart from the ideal model.',
'A sealed syringe compressed slowly can approximate Boyle’s law if heat exchange keeps temperature nearly constant. A tyre warmed at nearly fixed volume can show higher pressure. A flexible balloon may expand when warmed, but rubber tension, changing gas amount or water vapour can make the simple model less accurate.','gas'),
'y10/chemistry/acids-and-bases':(
'In this aqueous course, acids are described through production of H₃O⁺, often abbreviated H⁺, while soluble hydroxide bases provide OH⁻. A base need not be a soluble hydroxide. Indicators provide evidence about a solution under specified conditions; their colour is a measurement clue, not a complete description of every chemical present.',
'Strength concerns how far an acid or base ionises or reacts with water; concentration concerns how much was added per quantity of solution. Neutralisation compares reacting amounts, so begin with the balanced equation before comparing volumes. pH is logarithmic, and an indicator endpoint is an observed range rather than an exact definition of equivalence.',
'Vinegar concentration can be investigated by titration with a base of known concentration. A solution with 0.0020 mol of a monoprotic acid needs 0.0020 mol of hydroxide for complete neutralisation, not an arbitrary equal volume. Salt preparation also requires choosing a method suited to solubility and the reactants.','dilution'),
'y10/chemistry/metals':(
'An element contains atoms with one proton number; a compound contains elements chemically combined in a defined composition. Metals have structures and bonding that help explain properties such as electrical conduction and malleability. A shiny appearance alone is not a definition of a metal.',
'In a simplified reactivity series, a more reactive metal more readily loses electrons in the compared reactions. It can displace a less reactive metal from appropriate salt solutions. The medium, temperature and protective oxide layers can change observations. Extracting a metal from its compound requires a suitable chemical or electrochemical process; a more reactive metal is often harder to obtain from a stable compound.',
'Iron production uses reduction of iron oxides, while aluminium production needs electrolysis of an appropriate molten electrolyte. Alloys combine useful properties: steel can be made stronger through composition and structure. An alloy is not simply a new element with its own single proton number.',None),
'y10/chemistry/redox':(
'Redox describes paired changes involving electrons. Oxidation is electron loss and reduction is electron gain. Oxidation states help keep accounts even when electrons are not literally transferred as isolated particles. A half-equation must conserve both atoms and charge.',
'An electrolytic cell uses an external source to drive a reaction. Reduction occurs at the cathode and oxidation at the anode; in electrolysis the cathode is negative. Ions move through the electrolyte and electrons through metallic wires. Molten salts and aqueous solutions can produce different substances because water introduces competing reactions.',
'Electroplating deposits metal by reducing ions onto a surface. The charge passed is current multiplied by time, but how much metal appears also depends on electrons per ion and efficiency. Rusting is another redox context: the metal oxidation is paired with a reduction process in its surroundings.','redox'),
'y10/chemistry/organic-chemistry':(
'Organic chemistry connects carbon structures with properties and transformations. Carbon commonly forms four bonds in the simple models used here. Read the complete structure and functional group: a molecular formula counts atoms but does not uniquely identify how they are joined.',
'An open-chain alkane has only single carbon–carbon bonds; an alkene has a C=C bond, and a diene has two. Distillation separates molecules by boiling behaviour; cracking changes bonds and produces new molecules while conserving atoms. A functional group such as –OH or –COOH can determine important patterns of reactivity, but the rest of the molecule also matters.',
'Refineries separate crude oil into fractions with boiling ranges and use reactions to obtain useful products. Polymers form long chains from smaller units. For the illustrative balance C₈H₁₈ → C₄H₆ + C₄H₁₀ + H₂, count 8 carbons and 18 hydrogens on each side; this is not a promise of a single industrial cracking product.','organic'),
'y10/physics/electricity':(
'Charge is a property of matter, and current is the rate at which charge passes a point. Voltage is energy transferred per unit charge between points, not the amount of current stored in a wire. Resistance describes the relationship between voltage and current for a component under defined conditions.',
'A circuit needs an appropriate closed path for sustained current. Series components share current; parallel branches share voltage between the same two nodes. Use V = IR for an ohmic resistor at constant temperature. Coulomb’s law describes electrostatic force under a different model, and electrical safety requires distinguishing excess current from an imbalance caused by leakage.',
'A 2 kW heater used for 3 hours transfers 6 kWh of energy. A bill charges for energy, not simply the number of appliances or the supply voltage. A fuse and an RCD respond to different faults, which is why one protective device does not automatically replace another.','circuit'),
'y10/physics/waves':(
'A wave is a travelling disturbance. In a mechanical wave, particles can oscillate around their positions while the disturbance transfers energy. Amplitude measures maximum displacement, frequency counts oscillations per second and wavelength measures spacing between equivalent neighbouring points.',
'Wave speed relates frequency and wavelength through v = fλ. Sound is a longitudinal mechanical disturbance and needs a medium. Electromagnetic radiation can travel through a vacuum. Reflection, refraction, diffraction and interference are different behaviours; use the boundary conditions and the medium to decide which explanation fits an observation.',
'An echo uses a reflected sound pulse, and its travel time covers the outward and return journey. A 0.20 s echo at 340 m/s corresponds to a reflector 34 m away, not 68 m. A passing siren also illustrates Doppler shift: received frequency can change without the source changing its own emission frequency.','wave'),
'y10/physics/optics':(
'A ray represents a direction in which light travels in the geometrical model. A normal is a line perpendicular to a surface, and reflection and refraction angles are measured from it. An image can be real, where rays converge, or virtual, where their backward extensions appear to meet.',
'Refraction occurs when propagation speed changes across a boundary; frequency stays the same at a stationary boundary. Total internal reflection requires higher-to-lower refractive index and a sufficiently large incidence angle. Thin-lens equations relate object distance, image distance and focal length under approximations. Decide image type and sign convention before interpreting a calculator answer.',
'A camera needs a focused real image on its sensor, while a magnifying glass commonly gives a virtual image to the eye. With a converging lens of focal length 10 cm and object distance 30 cm, a screen at 15 cm captures a real inverted image half the object’s height.','lens'),
'y10/physics/thermal':(
'Temperature describes thermal state; heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. Internal energy is not the same as temperature or heat. Two objects at the same temperature can contain very different amounts of internal energy because their masses and substances differ.',
'For a temperature change without a phase transition, use Q = mcΔT with an appropriate specific heat capacity. During a transition at fixed pressure, latent heat can change state without raising the temperature of a pure substance. Conduction, convection and radiation provide different transfer mechanisms and can act together in the same situation.',
'Heating 0.50 kg of water by 20 °C requires about 42 kJ using c = 4200 J/(kg °C), ignoring the container and losses. A kettle supplies more energy if some heats its casing or escapes to the surroundings. Insulation changes transfer rates, not the definition of temperature.','thermal'),
'y10/physics/kinematics':(
'Motion is described relative to an origin, a positive direction and a clock. Position tells where an object is; displacement compares its final and initial positions. Average speed uses total distance, while average velocity uses displacement. Acceleration describes a change in velocity, including a change of direction.',
'The slope of a position–time graph is velocity; the slope of a velocity–time graph is acceleration, and its signed area is displacement. Use one shared clock in catch-up problems. A delayed object stays at its starting position before release and then moves for t minus its delay. Free-fall and projectile models need their assumptions, particularly negligible air resistance.',
'If A starts at 20 m at 3 m/s and B starts from 0 m two seconds later at 5 m/s, equal positions give 20 + 3t = 5(t − 2). They meet at t = 15 s and x = 65 m. B has moved for 13 s; equal meeting position does not mean equal distances travelled.','motion'),
'y10/physics/dynamics':(
'A force is an interaction acting on a chosen object. Draw only those interactions in its free-body diagram, not a force called motion. Weight is gravitational force, mass measures inertia, and contact forces depend on the surroundings. Newton’s third-law partners act on different objects.',
'Acceleration follows the vector sum of forces, Fnet = ma. Zero net force means constant velocity, which can be nonzero. Static friction adjusts up to a limit, while kinetic friction models sliding. On an incline, resolve forces relative to the surface. For connected bodies, consistent system boundaries and shared motion constraints help organise the equations.',
'A car travelling at steady speed can have a nonzero driving force balanced by resistance. During braking it may still move forward while its acceleration points backward. A 5 kg body with 20 N right, 5 N left and 5 N friction left has a 10 N net force and 2 m/s² acceleration right.','forces'),
'y12/maths/essentials':(
'A calculator returns results for the expression and settings you give it. Your first task is to translate a mathematical statement accurately: grouping, negative signs, fractions and angle units all matter. A long decimal display does not make an input or a model correct.',
'Estimate before calculating, enter brackets deliberately and keep enough precision for later steps. Distinguish a stored value from the rounded number shown in a written answer. For trigonometry, check radians or degrees; for a graph, check the viewing window and the domain before concluding that a feature is absent.',
'If a model predicts about 200 people and a calculation returns 0.2, check units and scale before copying it. A calculator can support a decision, but the answer needs interpretation: what quantity was found, what units belong to it and whether it is sensible.',None),
'y12/maths/number-and-algebra':(
'Algebra expresses relationships between quantities so they can be manipulated and solved. Powers describe repeated factors, logarithms undo exponentiation, and sequences describe ordered patterns. A symbol is meaningful only after its quantity, domain and any units are defined.',
'An equation asks for values that make two sides equal. A numerical solver can find a candidate in a region, but it does not automatically prove that all solutions have been found or that each is valid in the original domain. Financial and sequence models also require careful interpretation of time periods and rates.',
'With annual growth of 5%, 1000 becomes 1000 × 1.05ⁿ after n years in a simple compound-growth model. A logarithm can estimate when a target is reached, but the required whole year may need rounding upward. Arithmetic growth by a fixed addition is a different model.',None),
'y12/maths/functions':(
'A function assigns one output to each permitted input. The domain describes allowed inputs and the range describes resulting outputs. A formula, table and graph give different views of the same relationship, and the same-looking curve can represent different quantities depending on the axes.',
'A root is an input where the output is zero, while an intersection solves equality between two function values. A turning point concerns local change in a function, not simply where it crosses an axis. Set the window and inspect relevant intervals, and remember that restrictions or asymptotes can make a displayed connection misleading.',
'For a height model h(t), solving h(t) = 0 can predict a landing time, while a maximum predicts a peak height. A negative root might be mathematically valid but outside the physical time interval. For prices and demand, an intersection has a different interpretation although the graphical method is similar.','functions'),
'y12/maths/geometry-and-trigonometry':(
'Geometry connects lengths, angles and position. A trigonometric ratio compares side lengths in a right-angled triangle for a specified angle. An inverse trigonometric function retrieves an angle from a ratio, but its principal answer may not describe every possible angle in a larger problem.',
'Choose a diagram and label known quantities before selecting a formula. Degree and radian measures describe the same turn using different units: 180° = π radians. A calculator mode must match the inputs. Scale drawings are evidence for a plan, not a substitute for exact relationships or the required conditions of a theorem.',
'To estimate the height of a tower on level ground, a measured elevation angle and horizontal distance can define a right triangle. If the observer’s eye is above ground, add that height to the calculated rise. A correct tangent calculation can still give an incomplete answer if the model omits the observer.',None),
'y12/maths/statistics-and-probability':(
'Statistics describes and draws conclusions from data. A variable records a characteristic, a population is the group of interest and a sample is a subset observed. A mean measures one kind of centre; a standard deviation describes spread. Neither captures every feature of a distribution.',
'Choose a method suited to the variable and model. Sample and population standard deviations answer different questions. A probability model requires assumptions, such as independent trials and constant success probability for a binomial count. A p-value describes compatibility with a specified null model; it is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true.',
'A school survey can show association between study time and results, but a fitted regression alone does not prove that one causes the other. Selection of participants, measurement quality and other variables matter. Predicting far beyond the observed data adds an extrapolation risk.',None),
'y12/maths/calculus':(
'Calculus studies change and accumulation. A derivative describes an instantaneous rate of change, which can be approached through slopes over smaller intervals. A definite integral describes signed accumulation over an interval. The units depend on the original function and the variable of integration.',
'A numerical derivative or integral is an approximation under a method and settings, not an automatic exact proof. A stationary point has derivative zero, but further reasoning is needed to classify it. Signed area below an axis contributes negatively; total geometric area or total distance can require splitting intervals and using magnitudes.',
'If velocity is given in metres per second as a function of seconds, its definite integral gives displacement in metres. To find total distance, account for changes of direction. If a cost function is differentiated with respect to production quantity, the derivative has the interpretation and units of marginal cost.',None)
}

# Extra conceptual lessons where there is no existing stand-alone theory manifest.
EXTRA={
'y7/maths/algebra':[
 ('Translate an object story','Let x name the unknown count in one identical pen box. Adding three loose pens gives x + 3. Let y name the count in a different clip box: x + y keeps two potentially different quantities. Changing the letter definition midway changes the expression’s meaning.',['With x = 20 and y = 12, x + y = 20 + 12 = 32.','Two x boxes contain 2x = 40, so x + y does not generally equal 2x.']),
 ('Collect by variable and power','Like terms must have exactly the same variable part. Coefficients count copies and can be added, including negative coefficients. The variable and exponent stay unchanged during collection. Shapes can help identify groups, but colour does not establish algebraic equivalence.',['3x + 2x − x = (3 + 2 − 1)x = 4x.','2x² + 5x² = 7x², while x² + x³ stays as two unlike terms.']),
 ('Multiply every item in a bag','A bracket groups the contents of one bag. An outside factor counts identical copies of the entire group. Distributing multiplies every term, including its sign; it does not create an extra multiplication between the terms already inside.',['3(x + 2) = (x + 2) + (x + 2) + (x + 2).','Collecting gives 3x + 6.','For subtraction: 3(x − 2) = 3x − 6.'])],
'y9/science/circulatory-system':[
 ('Follow the double circuit','Blood returning from the body enters the right atrium, passes to the right ventricle and goes through pulmonary arteries to the lungs. It returns through pulmonary veins to the left atrium, enters the left ventricle and leaves through the aorta to the body. The two circuits are linked, not separate blood supplies.',['Body → venae cavae → right atrium → right ventricle.','Pulmonary arteries → lungs → pulmonary veins.','Left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body.']),
 ('Understand chambers and valves','Atria receive blood at relatively low pressure; ventricles pump it into vessels. Valves open and close because of pressure differences and help prevent backward flow. The septum separates the two sides, which normally carry blood with different oxygen contents.',['During ventricular contraction, atrioventricular valves close.','When ventricular pressure exceeds arterial pressure, outlet valves open.','Valve movement is caused by changing pressure, not by a decision to pull blood through.']),
 ('Connect structure to transport','The left ventricle usually needs a thicker muscular wall to generate higher systemic pressure. Arteries have walls suited to their pressure conditions; capillaries form thin exchange surfaces; veins return blood at lower pressure, often aided by valves and body movement.',['A pulmonary artery still carries blood away from the heart, despite its lower oxygen content.','Pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood from the lungs.','Blood with lower oxygen is still red; diagram colours are a labelling convention.'])],
'y9/science/breathing-system':[
 ('Trace the route and separate the processes','Air passes through the trachea, bronchi and smaller bronchioles to alveoli. Ventilation moves air, gas exchange moves gases across a surface, and cellular respiration transfers energy chemically in cells. A structure can support more than one of these processes without making them identical.',['Air enters the airways and reaches the alveoli.','Oxygen diffuses across the exchange barrier into blood.','Cells use oxygen in aerobic respiration and produce carbon dioxide.']),
 ('Explain inhalation through pressure','Contraction of the diaphragm makes it flatter and increases thoracic volume. External intercostal action also helps expand the chest. Lung pressure becomes slightly lower than atmospheric pressure, and air flows in. Quiet exhalation mainly uses elastic recoil as these muscles relax.',['Increase chest volume → lower lung pressure relative to outside.','Air flows down the pressure difference.','A model piston illustrates the pressure idea but is not a literal diaphragm inside the lung.']),
 ('Use gradients to explain gas exchange','A large total alveolar area, a thin barrier and maintained partial-pressure differences support diffusion. Ventilation renews alveolar air and circulation renews blood. Oxygen and carbon dioxide move in opposite net directions under normal conditions because their gradients differ.',['Higher oxygen partial pressure in alveolar air favours oxygen transfer to blood.','Higher carbon dioxide partial pressure in incoming blood favours transfer to alveolar air.','Diffusion does not require the lung to pump individual oxygen molecules across the barrier.'])],
'y9/science/gas-laws':[
 ('Boyle–Mariotte: pressure and volume','For a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, PV is constant. Halving available volume increases collision frequency per area in the ideal model, so absolute pressure doubles. Temperature control matters: rapid compression may warm a real sample.',['At 100 kPa and 40 mL, PV = 4000 kPa·mL.','At 20 mL, P = 4000/20 = 200 kPa.','The new pressure is absolute, not a gauge value.']),
 ('Charles: volume and absolute temperature','At fixed pressure and gas amount, V/T is constant when T is measured in kelvin. A movable boundary lets the gas expand while pressure stays controlled. Doubling a Celsius reading does not usually double the absolute temperature.',['Convert 27 °C to about 300 K.','At constant pressure, warming from 300 K to 330 K multiplies volume by 330/300 = 1.10.','A 50 mL sample becomes 55 mL in the ideal model.']),
 ('Pressure law: heating in a fixed volume','For a sealed rigid container holding an ideal gas, P/T is constant. Higher absolute temperature increases mean particle kinetic energy and pressure. A real tyre approximates fixed volume over a limited range; materials and failure cannot be inferred from the gas law alone.',['An ideal gas at 100 kPa and 300 K warms to 330 K.','At constant volume, P = 100 × 330/300 = 110 kPa.','Use the gas model to predict trends, not an actual tyre bursting temperature.'])]
}
EXTRA.update({
'y12/maths/essentials':[
 ('Translate the expression before pressing keys','The displayed result belongs to the entered expression, not to what you intended to enter. Group a whole numerator or denominator with brackets, and distinguish a negative number from a subtraction operation. A fraction bar groups expressions above and below it.',['For (3 + 5)/(2 × 4), evaluate the grouped numerator and denominator: 8/8 = 1.','Entering 3 + 5/2 × 4 describes a different expression.','Estimate the size first so an unexpected result prompts an input check.']),
 ('Control angle units, domain and window','Degrees divide a turn into 360 parts; radians relate arc length to radius. A trigonometric input must match the calculator mode. A graph window only shows part of a function: a missing intersection may lie outside it or outside a permitted domain.',['sin(30°) = 0.5.','sin(π/6 radians) = 0.5, but sin(30 radians) is a different value.','Before interpreting a graph of √x, remember its real-number domain x ≥ 0.']),
 ('Keep precision until the final interpretation','Rounding an intermediate value can change later calculations. Store or reuse unrounded values where practical, then round the final answer according to the question and context. Exact forms such as fractions or radicals retain information that a short decimal may omit.',['Use 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3 = 1 rather than 0.33 + 0.33 + 0.33 = 0.99.','A required minimum count of buses is rounded upward, not just to the nearest integer.','Include units and explain what the result represents.'])],
'y12/maths/number-and-algebra':[
 ('Powers and logarithms undo each other','If bˣ = a with b > 0 and b ≠ 1, then log_b(a) = x. A logarithm asks for an exponent, so its real input must be positive. Exponent and logarithm rules follow from products and powers; they do not turn a sum into a sum of logarithms.',['2³ = 8, so log₂(8) = 3.','Solve 2ˣ = 10 using x = ln(10)/ln(2) ≈ 3.322.','Check by substituting the value back into the original equation.']),
 ('Recognise arithmetic and geometric change','An arithmetic sequence has a constant difference between successive terms. A geometric sequence has a constant ratio when that ratio is defined. Identify the starting term and whether the index counts terms or elapsed periods before selecting a formula.',['For 2, 5, 8, … the nth term is 2 + 3(n − 1).','For 200, 208, 216.32, … the nth term is 200 × 1.04ⁿ⁻¹.','Adding 4 each time would be a different model from increasing by 4%.']),
 ('Solve a model and respect its context','Numerical solving can provide a value where an expression is zero or two expressions are equal. Check the original domain, inspect relevant intervals and substitute to verify. The practical interpretation may require an inequality or whole-number answer after solving the equality.',['For 1000 × 1.05ᵗ = 1500, t = ln(1.5)/ln(1.05) ≈ 8.31 years.','If interest is credited only at yearly endpoints, the first whole year meeting the target is year 9.','State the assumed compounding rule before applying the result.'])],
'y12/maths/functions':[
 ('Read a function as an input–output rule','Writing f(x) names the output corresponding to input x; it is not the product f × x. The domain restricts valid inputs. A graph is a set of pairs (x,f(x)), so an axis label and scale are part of the meaning.',['For f(x) = x² − 4, f(3) = 9 − 4 = 5.','For g(x) = √(x − 2), real inputs require x ≥ 2.','A point (3,5) belongs to f even if it is outside the current viewing window.']),
 ('Distinguish roots from intersections','A root solves f(x) = 0, so it is an x-axis crossing or touch where defined. An intersection of f and g solves f(x) = g(x). A numerical answer needs a chosen interval or starting region and a check that the plotted behaviour is not a discontinuity.',['For f(x) = x² − 4, roots are x = −2 and x = 2.','The intersection of y = x² and y = 2x satisfies x² = 2x, giving x = 0 or 2.','The corresponding intersection points are (0,0) and (2,4), not just the x values.']),
 ('Interpret turning points and models','A local maximum or minimum concerns nearby function values. A plotted turning point should be checked within the relevant domain and scale. A mathematical function can be evaluated beyond its useful modelling interval, so ask what each input and output means.',['For h(t) = −5t² + 20t + 1, complete the square: h(t) = −5(t − 2)² + 21.','The maximum model height is 21 at t = 2.','For a physical height story, negative times or times after landing may be outside the intended domain.'])],
'y12/maths/geometry-and-trigonometry':[
 ('Choose the right triangle and ratio','For a specified angle in a right-angled triangle, sine compares opposite to hypotenuse, cosine adjacent to hypotenuse and tangent opposite to adjacent. The opposite and adjacent labels depend on the chosen angle. The hypotenuse is always opposite the right angle.',['With horizontal distance 20 m and elevation angle 30°, rise = 20 tan(30°) ≈ 11.55 m.','If eye height is 1.60 m, the model tower height is about 13.15 m.','The assumptions include level ground and an angle measured from the horizontal.']),
 ('Use degrees and radians consistently','A radian is the angle subtended by an arc equal in length to the radius. A full circle measures 2π radians, equal to 360°. The formulas s = rθ and sector area = ½r²θ require θ in radians.',['60° = π/3 radians.','At radius 6 cm, arc length for 60° is 6 × π/3 = 2π cm.','Entering 60 into s = rθ without converting would not describe the same angle.']),
 ('Check conditions before choosing a formula','Pythagoras applies to right-angled triangles; sine and cosine rules extend to more general triangles. Draw and label the known data, identify possible ambiguous cases and test whether the answer fits the diagram and triangle constraints.',['For sides a = 5, b = 7 and included angle C = 60°, c² = 5² + 7² − 2(5)(7)cos60° = 39.','Therefore c = √39 ≈ 6.245.','The side length is positive and lies between |7 − 5| and 7 + 5.'])],
'y12/maths/statistics-and-probability':[
 ('Describe data before fitting a model','A data summary should describe centre, spread and shape, and consider unusual values. A mean uses every value; a median uses ordered position. A population standard deviation describes the whole listed population, while a sample estimate uses a different convention.',['For 2, 3, 4, 5, 16 the mean is 6 and the median is 4.','The large value pulls the mean upward.','A single average hides the difference between a tight cluster and a widely spread set.']),
 ('Match the probability model to the experiment','A binomial model counts successes in a fixed number of independent trials with constant success probability. A normal model is continuous and uses a mean and standard deviation. A calculator distribution command does not establish whether the assumptions are appropriate.',['For X binomial with n = 5 and p = 0.2, P(X = 0) = 0.8⁵ = 0.32768.','Therefore P(X ≥ 1) = 1 − 0.32768 = 0.67232.','Using a cumulative probability requires translating the inequality carefully.']),
 ('Use regression without inventing causation','Regression models association between variables and can support prediction within an appropriate range. Residuals compare observations with model predictions. Correlation alone does not establish cause; a strong fit can still reflect confounding, selection or a limited data range.',['If a fitted cost rule is C = 4 + 2x, the intercept is a fixed model cost and the slope is 2 cost units per x unit.','An observation C = 13 at x = 4 has residual 13 − 12 = 1.','Predicting for an x far beyond the data is extrapolation.']),
 ('Interpret inference in the stated model','A p-value is a probability, under a null model, of data at least as incompatible in the chosen test direction. It is not the probability that the null is true. A frequentist confidence procedure is assessed by repeated-sampling coverage, not by assigning a probability to a fixed parameter after observing one interval.',['A p-value of 0.03 is compared with a prespecified significance level.','At level 0.05 it may lead to rejection under the test conditions; at level 0.01 it would not.','State the assumptions, conclusion in context and limits rather than saying a hypothesis has been proved.'])],
'y12/maths/calculus':[
 ('From average rate to instantaneous rate','An average rate compares output change with input change over an interval. A derivative is the limiting rate as that interval shrinks, when the limit exists. On a graph it is a tangent slope, not the height of the graph at the same input.',['For s(t) = t², the average rate from 2 to 2+h is ((2+h)² − 4)/h = 4 + h.','As h approaches zero, the rate approaches 4.','If s is metres and t seconds, this derivative is 4 m/s at t = 2 s.']),
 ('Classify a stationary point','Solving f′(x) = 0 identifies stationary candidates, but zero derivative does not by itself guarantee a maximum or minimum. Examine how the derivative changes sign or use a suitable second-derivative argument. Also inspect endpoints when finding an interval’s global extreme.',['For f(x) = x², f′(x) = 2x is negative before 0 and positive after 0, so x = 0 is a minimum.','For g(x) = x³, g′(0) = 0 but the function continues increasing through 0.','A calculator’s stationary result still needs classification.']),
 ('Distinguish accumulation, area and distance','A definite integral gives signed accumulation. Contributions below the axis have negative sign, so a net result can be smaller than total geometric area. For velocity, integrate signed values for displacement and magnitudes for total distance. Split at sign changes when needed.',['For v(t) = t − 2 on 0 ≤ t ≤ 4, negative and positive triangular areas cancel: displacement is 0.','Each triangle has area 2, so total distance is 4 when units are m/s and seconds.','Zero displacement does not mean the object stayed still.'])]
})
